 ## Background
-The concept of representing dynamic scenes using neural radiance fields originated with the need to model non-rigid deformations, such as those found in surgical tissues or human motion. Early work, such as that described in [S1], applied this to robotic surgery, representing deformable tissues as a canonical field with a time-dependent displacement field. Concurrently, other approaches like TöRF [S2, unofficial] extended these concepts to integrate Time-of-Flight (ToF) data, using separate static and dynamic networks to handle moving cameras and dynamic scenes. These methods established the foundation for current dynamic NeRF architectures by demonstrating how displacement fields and canonical spaces could effectively model temporal changes.
